            <ArticleTitle>Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty in diabetic patients: an effective treatment modality.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>204-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA) was performed for nonhealing ulcers, severe claudication, or rest pain in 10 patients with diabetes mellitus. Twenty-two atherosclerotic lesions were dilated in 15 extremities, including 16 iliofemoral arteries and 6 superficial femoral arteries. The procedure was technically successful in all patients and 8 of 9 (89%) with clinical follow-up showed improvement clinically after the procedure. Diabetes is not a contraindication to PTA; rather this modality can be used effectively to control and treat peripheral vascular insufficiency in diabetic patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
